-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
# floor30-atlas-dot-slashless-operand — QUESTION (added mechanic): `. helpers.sh`, with no `./`.
# POSIX says a slashless operand is looked up along PATH, and PATH here does not contain the current
# directory. Do the floor binaries fall back to the cwd anyway, or does the load fail?
#
# Why the inclusion design needs it, and why it is not on the conductor's list: `rul-dot-resolves-as-sh`
# declares the slashless operand UNSUPPORTED at v0 and stops there — but "unsupported" is a statement
# about what Dorc resolves, not about what happens when an admin writes it and the artifact ships it
# verbatim. If a real shell silently finds the cwd copy, then a book that works by accident today
# breaks the moment an artifact runs from a generation directory; if it fails, it fails as a special
# builtin and the previous case's answer applies. Either way the engine's silence is only safe if
# the shells agree, so this is a floor question rather than a policy one.
#
# The guarded cell comes first so its answer survives whatever the bare one does.
printf 'book-before\n'
. helpers.sh || printf 'book: guarded caught status=[%s]\n' "$?"
printf 'book-after-guarded: helper=[%s]\n' "${slashless_helper_mark-unset}"
. helpers.sh
printf 'book-after-bare\n'

-- helpers.sh --
slashless_helper_mark=found-via-path-search
printf 'helper-file-ran\n'

-- expected.emitted --
book-before

-- expected.ran --

-- replay --
$ dorc --book=book.sh --results -
loading: warning[slashless-source-searches-path]: [unwritten:
slashless-source-searches-path]
      [ book.sh:16:1
   16 | . helpers.sh || printf 'book: guarded caught status=[%s]\n' "$?"
      | \__________/
loading: warning[slashless-source-searches-path]: [unwritten:
slashless-source-searches-path]
      [ book.sh:18:1
   18 | . helpers.sh
      | \__________/
#!/bin/sh
# dorc probe (read-only): checks per-SITE convergence, mutates nothing.
# When run, emits one results-record per site on stdout (the return channel):
#   site <leafid> effect=<holds|absent|cant-tell> rc=<n>
# effect is derived from the probe command's rc (0=holds, 1=absent, else cant-tell);
# rc is the raw PROBE-command status (opaque to Dorc -- the record is the out-of-band lane).

printf 'dorc-records/1 nonce=dorc attempt=1 host=localhost book=68649c8840093434ef82e773826eecec7e1e8acc334e800f0142276de065b13a sites=0 @@dorc@@\n'
# site:0 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:1 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:2 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:3 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:4 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:5 unresolvable-no-probe
printf 'dorc-records-end/1 nonce=dorc @@dorc@@\n'
probe: note[site-unresolvable]: sm 2 sites run unprobed (no read-only check
could be shipped): `. helpers.sh`, `. helpers.sh` -- run `dorc why` for the
per-site detail (the apply runs each anyway, to stay safe)
      [ book.sh:16:1
   16 | . helpers.sh || printf 'book: guarded caught status=[%s]\n' "$?"
      | \__________/

   = help:  sm site runs `. helpers.sh`
hint: '.' (line 16) is unmodeled: it is the first wall -- an oracle vouching its convergence would elide it when converged; 1 more unmodeled wall -- dorc why
dorc: run `dorc why` for the per-site cause-chains, or `dorc why book.sh:N` to query a source line
#!/bin/sh
# dorc apply: the book, with already-converged/dead lines elided (value-preserving stand-in).

